# LOGICVID.SKILL
Version: 1.0

Purpose:

Construct a chain of philosophical investigations that progressively eliminates reducible concepts until only genuinely irreducible primitives remain.

This is NOT philosophy education.

This is ontology reduction.

The output should resemble Euclid, Spinoza, Gödel, or modern theoretical physics more than a lecture.

Every episode must permanently change the ontology.

If nothing new has been established, the episode has failed.

---

## Core Principle

Every concept introduced into the ontology is guilty until proven irreducible.

The burden of proof is always:

Can this concept be reduced to something simpler?

If yes:

delete it.

If no:

keep it.

Proceed.

---

## The Workflow

Each Logicvid contains exactly one question.

Never answer two.

Never drift.

The question should emerge inevitably from the previous episode.

---

## Episode Structure

# 1. Point of Contention

Begin at the frontier.

Not history.

Not definitions.

State the exact point where current philosophy or science can no longer proceed.

Example:

"Physics explains fields.

Abhinavagupta explains manifestation.

Neither explains why manifestation possesses self-articulation."

Immediately formalize.

---

# 2. Primitive

Declare the candidate primitive.

Example

Presence

Manifestation

Difference

Memory

Recognition

Agency

Freedom

NOT

Consciousness

Mind

Reality

Those are usually composite terms.

---

# 3. Abhinavagupta First

Always.

Never begin elsewhere.

Reconstruct his actual argument from primary texts.

Not summaries.

Not "Shaivism says..."

Formalize each premise.

Example

A1

Experience presupposes manifestation.

A2

Manifestation cannot be externally illuminated.

A3

Therefore manifestation is intrinsic.

Only then move on.

---

# 4. Reduction Attempts

Attempt to eliminate the primitive.

Use only the strongest reductions.

Never include thinkers merely because they are famous.

Every reduction must have the form

Primitive = f(X)

Examples

Presence = Experience

Presence = Attention

Presence = Integration

Presence = Agency

Presence = Information

Memory = Prediction

Recognition = Memory

Freedom = Complexity

etc.

Every reduction either succeeds or fails.

Formal proof only.

---

# 5. Strongest Rival

Choose ONE.

Not five.

Find the strongest contemporary theory.

Sometimes:

Russellian Monism

Sometimes:

Active Inference

Sometimes:

Quantum Field Theory

Sometimes:

Michael Levin

Sometimes:

Dharmakirti

Sometimes:

Nanavira

Sometimes:

none.

Do not reuse favourite thinkers.

They must earn inclusion.

---

# 6. Scientific Pressure

Research first.

Current literature only.

Ask

"What has modern science actually explained?"

Then

"What remains?"

Never use science decoratively.

Science exists only to attack or support one premise.

---

# 7. Theorem

State exactly what has survived.

Not opinions.

Formal result.

Example

Presence ≠ Experience

Presence ≠ Attention

Presence may survive reduction.

---

# 8. Residual

One thing remains unexplained.

This becomes the next episode.

Never choose topics manually.

The ontology chooses.

---

## Style

No introductions.

No scene setting.

No storytelling.

Every paragraph advances the proof.

Maximum compression.

Every sentence should either

introduce

prove

refute

or conclude.

---

## Formality

Use equations constantly.

Treat philosophy like theoretical physics.

Avoid vague prose.

---

## Research Rules

Never search

"what is consciousness"

Instead search

"arguments against X"

"current frontier"

"strongest objection"

"primary source"

"recent review"

"formal theory"

Every episode should contain

primary sources

modern scholarship

current science

---

## Thinker Selection

Abhinavagupta is permanent.

Everyone else rotates.

Do NOT repeatedly include

Dharmakirti

Levin

Active Inference

IIT

Predictive Processing

Only include them when they attack the primitive directly.

---

## Failure Modes

Never

- teach

- summarise

- compare traditions

- produce surveys

- brainstorm

- list opinions

- explain history

Instead

eliminate ontology.

---

## Success Criterion

At the end of each episode

the ontology contains

one fewer primitive

or

one primitive has survived every reduction.

Either outcome is progress.
